aude.ai&amp;parentOrigin=3Dhttps%3A%2F%2Fclaude.ai&amp;errorReportingMode=
=3Dparent&amp;formattedSpreadsheets=3Dtrue&amp;theme=3Dlight&amp;contentTyp=
e=3Dtext%2Fhtml#checklist">Checklist</a>
</nav>
<main>
<p>Do the "before you fly" items at home, on home Wi-Fi, with your Australi=
an SIM working. Several of them need an SMS code, and many apps and website=
s can't be downloaded once you're in China.</p>

<section id=3D"visa"><h2>1. Visa: not needed, with one date to check</h2>
<ul>
<li>Australian passport holders can enter China <b>visa-free for up to 30 d=
ays per entry</b> for tourism. Two weeks is well within that.</li>
</ul>
<div class=3D"warn"><b>The catch:</b> the visa-free scheme is officially co=
nfirmed only until <b>31 December 2026</b>. As of 20 November 2026 China ha=
dn't announced an extension into 2027. It has renewed every year so far, ea=
ch time in November. <b>If you arrive in 2027, check the Chinese Embassy we=
bsite for the extension notice before you fly.</b> If it hasn't come throug=
h, you'd need a tourist (L) visa.</div>
<ul>
<li>Passport: at least 6 months' validity left and a couple of blank pages.=
</li>
<li>Carry a printed or saved copy of your return flight and your first hote=
l booking in case immigration asks.</li>
<li><b>Arrival card:</b> fill it in online before you fly. It's free at <co=
de>s.nia.gov.cn/ArrivalCardFillingPC/</code>, in the "NIA 12367" app, or in=
 the Alipay mini-program. Save the QR code. Avoid any site that charges for=
 it. Paper cards and airport kiosks are still available as a fallback.</li>
</ul></section>

<section id=3D"internet"><h2>2. Phone and internet: the most important part=
</h2>
<p>On any Chinese network (hotel Wi-Fi, caf=C3=A9 Wi-Fi, a Chinese SIM) the=
se are <b>blocked</b>: Google (Search, Maps, Gmail, Drive, Translate, <b>Pl=
ay Store</b>), WhatsApp, Instagram, Facebook, YouTube, ChatGPT and most Wes=
tern news sites.</p>
<p><b>The fix is a data connection that routes outside China. You then don'=
t need a VPN.</b></p>
<ul>
<li><b>Option A: a travel eSIM</b> (Airalo, Holafly, Nomad, Saily, Trip.com=
 and similar). Pick a mainland China plan that says Google/WhatsApp work. I=
nstall it at home and switch it on when you land. To check your Samsung sup=
ports eSIM: Settings =E2=80=BA Connections =E2=80=BA SIM manager =E2=80=BA =
"Add eSIM".</li>
<li><b>Option B: Australian roaming</b> (Telstra, Optus or Vodafone interna=
tional day pass). It's dearer but needs no setup, and everything works beca=
use your traffic goes through Australia.</li>
<li><b>VPN: backup only</b>, for hotel Wi-Fi. Install, pay for and test it =
<b>in Australia</b>, because VPN sites and app downloads are blocked inside=
 China. VPNs got less reliable there in 2026.</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Samsung-specific:</b></p>
<ul>
<li>The Google Play Store won't load in China. <b>Install every app before =
you leave.</b></li>
<li>Samsung Wallet and Google Pay don't work for payments there. Use Alipay=
.</li>
<li>Keep your <b>Australian SIM active for SMS</b> (bank and Alipay codes).=
 If you use an eSIM for data, turn data roaming off on the Australian SIM s=
o you don't get a bill.</li>
<li>Public Wi-Fi often needs a Chinese phone number, which is one more reas=
on to rely on mobile data.</li>
</ul></section>

<section id=3D"pay"><h2>3. How to pay</h2>
<p>China is almost cashless. People pay by scanning QR codes everywhere, ev=
en at street stalls.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Alipay is your main tool.</b> Sign up with your Australian mobile nu=
mber, verify your passport and link a Visa or Mastercard. Visa is reported =
to be the most reliable. Make a small test payment before you depend on it.=
</li>
<li><b>WeChat:</b> install it too. It works as a backup wallet with foreign=
 cards, and it's how locals, guides and hotels will message you.</li>
<li><b>Link two different cards</b> in case one gets declined, and tell bot=
h banks you're travelling.</li>
<li><b>Fees:</b> small payments are generally free, but about 3% has applie=
d above =C2=A5200. Check in the app. A no-foreign-fee travel card helps.</l=
i>
<li><b>Cards directly:</b> fine at big hotels, malls and chains, rarely any=
where small.</li>
<li><b>Cash:</b> carry =C2=A5500=E2=80=931,000 in small notes as a backup. =
Airport and Bank of China ATMs take Australian cards.</li>
</ul></section>

<section id=3D"maps"><h2>4. Maps and getting around</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>Don't rely on Google Maps, even with an eSIM.</b> Its data is often =
out of date, and China's shifted coordinate system can put your blue dot hu=
ndreds of metres from where you really are.</li>
<li><b>Use Amap (Gaode Maps, =E9=AB=98=E5=BE=B7=E5=9C=B0=E5=9B=BE).</b> It =
has English, live traffic and proper public-transport routing.</li>
<li><b>Save every address in Chinese characters.</b> Paste them into Amap o=
r DiDi, and show them to drivers.</li>
<li><b>Taxis: DiDi.</b> Use the app or the DiDi mini-program inside Alipay,=
 which is in English and pays automatically. Avoid touts at airports and st=
ations.</li>
<li><b>Metro:</b> scan the transport QR code in Alipay at the gate (Alipay =
=E2=80=BA Transport).</li>
<li><b>Trains: your passport is your ticket.</b> Book on Trip.com or 12306 =
(both in English). Check <b>which station</b>, because big cities have seve=
ral (Beijing South, Beijing West, Shanghai Hongqiao=E2=80=A6). Allow 45=E2=
=80=9360 minutes for passport and security checks.</li>
</ul></section>

<section id=3D"apps"><h2>5. Apps to install before flying</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>Alipay</b>: payments, DiDi, metro, translator</li>
<li><b>WeChat</b>: messaging, backup payments</li>
<li><b>Amap</b>: maps</li>
<li><b>DiDi</b>: taxis (optional if you use it via Alipay)</li>
<li><b>Trip.com</b>: trains, flights, hotels</li>
<li><b>Google Translate</b> with the <b>Chinese offline pack downloaded</b>=
 (or Microsoft Translator)</li>
<li>Your eSIM app, plus a VPN if you've decided on one</li>
<li>Save offline copies of bookings, tickets and a passport scan on the pho=
ne itself, not only in Gmail or Google Drive</li>
</ul></section>

<section id=3D"passport"><h2>6. Passport and registration</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>Carry your passport at all times.</b> You need it for trains, hotels=
, museums and many attractions.</li>
<li>Big attractions (Forbidden City etc.) are booked against your passport =
number, so the name and number on your bookings must <b>exactly match</b> y=
our passport.</li>
<li>Hotels register you with the police automatically. If you stay in a pri=
vate home, you must register at the local police station within 24 hours.</=
li>
</ul></section>

<section id=3D"bits"><h2>7. Practical bits</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>Power:</b> 220V. Australian plugs fit most Chinese wall sockets, so =
you usually don't need an adapter.</li>
<li><b>Emergency numbers:</b></li>
</ul>
<div class=3D"em"><span>Police 110</span><span>Ambulance 120</span><span>Fi=
re 119</span></div>
<ul>
<li><b>Australian help:</b> Embassy in Beijing, Consulate-General in Shangh=
ai, and DFAT's 24-hour Consular Emergency Centre on <a href=3D"tel:+6126261=
3305">+61 2 6261 3305</a>. Register your trip on Smartraveller.</li>
<li>Get <b>travel insurance</b> and keep the policy saved offline.</li>
<li>Keep medication in its original packaging with a copy of the prescripti=
on.</li>
<li>Carry tissues and hand sanitiser. Many toilets don't supply paper.</li>
</ul></section>

<section id=3D"checklist"><h2>8. Before-you-fly checklist</h2>
<ul class=3D"check">
<li><label><input type=3D"checkbox"> Visa-free status checked (if you arriv=
e on or after 1 Jan 2027)</label></li>
<li><label><input type=3D"checkbox"> Passport valid 6+ months; bookings mat=
ch it exactly</label></li>
<li><label><input type=3D"checkbox"> Online arrival card done, QR code save=
d</label></li>
<li><label><input type=3D"checkbox"> eSIM installed or roaming pack on</lab=
el></li>
<li><label><input type=3D"checkbox"> Alipay verified, two cards linked, tes=
t payment made</label></li>
<li><label><input type=3D"checkbox"> WeChat installed and logged in</label>=
</li>
<li><label><input type=3D"checkbox"> Amap, DiDi, Trip.com and translator in=
stalled; offline Chinese pack downloaded</label></li>
<li><label><input type=3D"checkbox"> Addresses saved in Chinese characters<=
/label></li>
<li><label><input type=3D"checkbox"> Banks told; some cash on hand</label><=
/li>
<li><label><input type=3D"checkbox"> Insurance, Smartraveller, documents sa=
ved offline</label></li>
</ul>
<p class=3D"small">Ticks are saved on this phone only.</p></section>

<p class=3D"small">Sources: Chinese Consulate-General Adelaide (visa-free n=
otice), China National Immigration Administration, Beijing and Shanghai gov=
ernment English portals, and current travel/payment guides. Rules change, s=
o recheck the visa point close to departure.</p>
</main>



